.TH XUDU 1 "August 2026" "xudu" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
xudu \- a xanadoc editor, after Ted Nelson's OSMIC
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xudu
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fISTORE\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xudu
edits a
.IR xanadoc :
a document kept as an append-and-read-only record of operations rather than as
a file. Text that is typed goes into the author's permascroll at an address it
keeps forever, and each edit is recorded in the document's operations spool
under the state it produced.
.PP
The permascroll is the author's, not the document's. Everything typed into
every xanadoc goes into one of them, so a passage quoted from one document into
another is the same bytes at the same address rather than a copy \-\- which is
what makes the two detectably related afterwards. A store directory is
therefore an edit decision list: it names addresses and does not contain them,
and it is read against the permascroll it was written against. See
.B \-\-permascroll .
.PP
No version is stored. A state's name spells out the operations that rebuild it,
in Nelson's numbering where change 2a4 creates state 2a4, so 2a4 is reached by
replaying 1, 2, 2a1, 2a2, 2a3 and 2a4.
.PP
Going back to an earlier state and editing it starts a branch rather than
destroying what already followed, and deleting stops pointing at content
without removing it from the permascroll, so every state the document has ever
been in stays reachable.
.PP
Quoting a passage from one document into another inserts a pointer to the
address the original already uses, so there is one copy and two documents
showing it. Whether two documents share content is therefore a question about
addresses: it survives editing around the quotation, and two documents that
merely read the same are correctly not treated as sharing anything.
.PP
.I STORE
is the directory the document lives in. It is created if it is not there, and
defaults to
.IR xanadoc .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-import " \fIFILE\fR"
Read
.I FILE
into an empty store as its first operation. Refused on a store that already has
operations, since importing into one would be an ordinary edit and should be
made as one.
.TP
.BR \-\-torrent " \fIFILE\fR|\fIMAGNET\fR"
Make a torrent's content available for quoting. Repeatable.
.IP
A torrent's info hash is derived from its content, so it names that content
everywhere and permanently, and every piece carries a hash so what arrives can
be checked against what was named. A quotation into a torrent therefore means
the same thing to anyone who has the reference, and keeps meaning it after this
machine is gone.
.IP
A magnet link is accepted here too, in either the hex or base-32 spelling, but
it carries only the name: the piece hashes it must be verified against are in
the torrent's info dictionary, which a client normally fetches from the swarm.
A magnet whose metadata is not available is refused rather than recorded as a
reference that could never resolve.
.IP
A BEP 46 name is accepted here as well, written
.BR magnet:?xs=urn:btpk: \fIKEY\fR .
It is not a hash of anything: it is an ed25519 public key, and what it points
at is a signed entry in the DHT that its holder can move. That is what a
reference to something still being written has to be, since an info hash fixes
the content it names and appending would produce a different one.
.IP
The answer is believed only if it carries a signature made by the key in the
name, so the node that supplied it is not trusted, and neither is the DHT. It
resolves to an info hash, and from there nothing is different.
.TP
.BR \-\-dht\-node " \fIHOST\fR:\fIPORT\fR"
Introduce a DHT node. Repeatable. A DHT is joined by knowing somebody already
in it, and no public router is contacted unless it is named here, so resolving
a
.B btpk
name needs at least one.
.TP
.B \-\-private\-dht
Do not apply the public-DHT rule that limits the routing table to one node per
/8. On one private network every node is in the same /8, and the rule leaves a
DHT of one.
.TP
.B \-\-swarm
Fetch quoted content from BitTorrent peers rather than only from this disk.
.IP
Without it a reference resolves only when this machine already holds the bytes,
which is the dependency on one machine that addressing content by its hash
exists to remove. Only the pieces a quotation needs are requested, and only
whole pieces, since a piece hash covers a piece and says nothing about a
fragment of one. With a swarm a bare magnet link resolves too: the metadata it
lacks is fetched from a peer and accepted only if it hashes back to the name
the link carried.
.IP
Available only when the program was built with libtorrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-peer " \fIHOST\fR:\fIPORT\fR"
Introduce a peer known to hold some of the content. Repeatable. Needed only
when there is no tracker or DHT to find one through.
.TP
.BR \-\-torrent\-data " \fIDIR\fR"
Directory the torrent's files are in. The default is the directory the
.I .torrent
file itself is in, which is where a downloader usually leaves them.
.TP
.BR \-\-quote " \fIFILE\fR,\fIOFFSET\fR,\fILENGTH\fR"
Quote that range of the most recently given
.B \-\-torrent
into the document. A length of 0 means the rest of the file. Repeatable.
.IP
Nothing is copied: the document ends up holding a pointer to content addressed
by its own hash, so a store made entirely of quotations adds nothing to the
author's permascroll at all. Content that cannot be reached, or that does not
hash to what the
reference named, reads as nothing rather than as something plausible -- and the
rest of the document is unaffected, because failing to resolve a reference is
not the same as the document being corrupt.
.TP
.BR \-\-permascroll " \fIDIR\fR"
Bind this directory as the author's permascroll instead of
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/xudu/permascroll/default .
It is created if it is not there.
.IP
Every document opened in this run shares it, and a document records addresses
into whichever permascroll was bound when it was written. Opening a store
against a different one resolves its local spans against the wrong content, so
this is what a xanadoc written elsewhere \-\- a checked-in sample, another
machine's export \-\- needs alongside it.
.TP
.BR \-\-version\-id " \fIID\fR"
Open this microversion, for example
.BR 2a4 .
The default is the most recent state in the store.
.TP
.BR \-\-alongside " \fIID\fR"
Also open this microversion as a second document, for reading two states
against each other. Passages they share are shaded in both.
.TP
.B \-\-map
Start with the hypertime map shown.
.TP
.BR \-\-backend " \fINAME\fR"
Rendering backend:
.BR opengl " (the default), " opengles " or " vulkan .
The Vulkan backend is present only when the binary was built with it.
.TP
.BR \-\-font " \fIDESCRIPTION\fR"
Font, as a Pango font description, for example "Serif 16".
.TP
.BR \-\-fov " \fIDEGREES\fR"
Initial vertical field of view. Widening it brings more of the documents on
screen at once.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-asset\-dir
Report the directory the shaders and the icon were found in, then quit.
.TP
.B \-\-help\-all
Show every option, including those for driving a run without a person at the
keyboard, and describe them at length.
.SH KEYS
Commands take control, because a bare letter is text: the point of this program
is that typing is an edit, so it has to reach the document.
.TP
.BR ctrl\-b ", " ctrl\-n
Go back or forward in hypertime. Nothing is lost either way.
.TP
.B ctrl\-t
Quote the selection into a second document.
.TP
.B ctrl\-l
Attach a link to the selected content. A link names content rather than a
position, so it appears on every document quoting it.
.TP
.B ctrl\-m
Show or hide the hypertime map.
.TP
.B ctrl\-p
Print every state to the terminal.
.TP
.BR ctrl\-s ", " ctrl\-q
Write the document out; save and quit.
.TP
.B backspace
Stop pointing at the selection.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I STORE/ops.nodes
Every operation, as the fixed-size records the program holds them as, filed
under the state each produced. Opens with a header naming the format version
and the record size, so a file written in another shape is refused rather than
read as this one.
.TP
.I STORE/store.tables
The document's side tables, in one versioned binary container: the scroll
registry, the local media segments, the links, the author's current versions,
and the aliases and descriptions given to any of them.
.IP
The scroll registry is what gives a span's scroll number something behind it
\-\- a publisher's key, or nothing when the scroll has none \-\- and which
torrent carries which stretch of it. Which torrent carries a stretch changes
every time something is sealed, and changing it moves no address: that is what
addressing content by scroll rather than by container buys.
.IP
Binary, and not meant to be read with
.BR less .
.BR xudu\-dump (1)
renders any of it as text; that is the tool's purpose.
.TP
.I STORE/ops.export
Present only when
.B \-\-export\-osmic
was given: the same operations in canonical OSMIC text. Read back if
.I ops.nodes
is absent.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/xudu/permascroll/default/active.primedia
Every byte the author has ever typed, in any document, at the address it was
given. Not part of any store: see
.B \-\-permascroll
and the description above.
.TP
.I @DATADIR@/shaders
Shader sources loaded at run time.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GLEDITOR_ASSET_DIR
Directory to read the shaders and icon from, overriding the search.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gleditor (1)
.PP
OSMIC: <https://xanadu.com.au/ted/OSMIC/>
.SH AUTHOR
Written by the gleditor authors.
